A||||8||LivoniaFieroGT||stuck on rear rotors - how to pull||||1||
Z||000000||LivoniaFieroGT||08-02-2001||09:42 PM||||I'm helping a friend and we get the calipers of and go to pull the rotors off the shaft sleeve and they are stuck.  Tapping with a hammer and mallet on the back side only loosened piles of rust.  Is there a tool (name please) that will allow us to pull off with equal pressure around the circumference.  Am letting them sit the night out with rust penetrant on them.||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||08-02-2001||10:00 PM||||I don't know of any special tool......I always use a BFH.  Just whack one area with a big sledge hammer for all you're worth then rotate the rotor 90* and do it again, etc.  Eventually it will pop off.<P>Good luck. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||08-02-2001||10:19 PM||||think you talking about a gear/harmonic bal puller. If you cant get it off by hammer, you could prob rent a puller<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||PBJ||08-02-2001||10:24 PM||||Just keep hitting the rotor between the wheel nut studs, when they start to come loose the sound of the banging will change.  And is a good idea to let it soak.||||reg||1
Z||000004||k2m4||08-03-2001||09:32 AM||||hey dude use a sledge hammer ...you dont need a full swing just some persuasion and it will loosen up! i had the same problem last week. it will come loose just bang away!<P>------------------<BR>Kerm<BR>86 2M4<BR>86 1/2 GT||||reg||14
Z||000005||FieroSoul||08-03-2001||01:14 PM||||  Just to check another possiblity, if the rotors have never been off before they may still have the factory internal star washers on a couple of the studs.  Did you check for them.  If you have they can be removed by getting ahold of them with some pliers and twisting them off.  If you have already pulled them, I apologize for restating the obvious but they are sometimes hard to see especially with a lot of rust like you mentioned.||||reg||3
Z||000006||StuGood||08-03-2001||06:13 PM||||This won't help any, but your situation reminds me of a story  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">...<P>Was ready to do the (rear) brakes on my old 1982 Cavalier but couldn't get the drums off.  Wire brushed hub 'til it sparkled, loosened brake adjusters as far as they'd go, oiled, hammered, pried, etc., you name it - they wouldn't budge.<P>Hot, sweaty, tired and irritated now. Put wheels back on, drove car to Rapid Brake and Muffler.  Told the guy I just wanted them to get the rear drums off, asked how much that'd cost me.  He didn't answer my question, but said if I left the car there, he'd probably be able to do it sometime that day.  <P>I said that'd be fine.  I didn't care - I was still hot (and bothered).  If it cost too much I'd just let them have the stupid car.  Anyway, walked home, which helped my attitude considerably.  Later in the evening, before the shop's closing time, I gave them a call.  They had it done  - brake drums had been taken off and reinstalled.<P>Went to pick it up (using another car).  Asked the guy if he had any trouble.  Nope, he said, just used some special solvent they always use, and wacked it with a big hammer.  Came right off.<P>Asked what I owed him.  He kind of looked off in the distance, and then said, "We'll just call it a free brake inspection.  No charge."  After thanking him and taking the old Cav. home, I jacked it up and the drums came right off so I was able to finish the job!         <P>Nice people can change your whole attitude  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Songman||08-04-2001||05:08 PM||||The trick is a big rubber hammer.... Beat the devil out of them....||||reg||6
Z||000008||LivoniaFieroGT||08-04-2001||08:36 PM||||Whoa.... I tried the rubber mallet first and darn near severed my hand on the rebound..... tight quarters for that kind of recoil.<BR>Beat the crap out of the rotors and did the 90 degree rotation and after 20 or so hits the first on was off.  The other side went even easier once you realize what reckless abandon is.  Thanks for the advice, cause I was hesitant to hit it had.  Couldn't find the gear puller at the local Autozone or PepBoys.  So I bought the rubber mallet.||||reg||1
